Jim Miller wrote:

> I could go on.  It's best not to hold the game to
> too rigorous a level of consistency, but I also
> have to argue against those who try to make up a
> backstory that legitimizes every quirk of the
> game.  It gets too stultified and constructed.

There, I have to agree. It is sometimes a fun
intellectual exercise to try to make up a reason the
game works the way it does, but more often, it is
_not_ fun, the strained logic just produces more
questions than it gives answers, and the discussion
drags on interminably, as at present.

In the end, after all, the only legitimate
explanation for the detailed behavior of a game
feature is: "that's how the code works", full stop.
There's no need to make "real world logical"
behavior of code which was in the case written not
to match the real world, but "to make playing the
game fun".

In the case of the current discussion, NetHack once
_had_ "real world locks" with an assortment of keys
(eight of them comes somehow to mind), each lock
openable only by the matching key, but that feature
turned out to make for very tedious game play, and
the various flavors of keys ate up knapsack slots
and also ate up time to find the correct key for the
lock even given a full assortment of keys, out of
all proportion to their value to the player.
Fumbling four different times on average, to unlock
a door, while being pursued by a flock of giant ants
isn't "fun" gameplay.

So, now we have "locks" which keep out only the
honest, lockpicks and credit cards catering to the
vast horde of dishonest, with a single pattern of
key, whose only virtue is that the keys are scarce.

What shopkeepers, who don't visit one another's
shops except in pursuit of a fleeing shoplifter,
think of other shopkeepers having keys which work
also in all shops not their own, all chests, all
boxes, all doors, isn't of meaningful consequence to
the game, so why does the issue need to be resolved
"in the real world sense", shopkeepers being at best
and at worst software constructs anyway???

